course: dictionary
lesson: 260
title: Altogether: one total, completely, or on the whole
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Altogether is a small word with three main meanings. Read the words around it to find which one fits. «The shop sold 3 apples, 4 pears and 2 plums.» Add them up and you get the total: that is how many fruit there were altogether.
explain: Sometimes altogether means completely, with nothing left. «The noise stopped altogether.» Nothing was left of the noise, not even a tiny bit. Try putting completely in its place: if the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
explain: Altogether can also mean on the whole, when you think about everything together. «The trip had some rain, but altogether it was a great day.» The rain was a small problem; when you think of the whole day, it was great. Ask yourself: is this about a total, about completely, or about the whole picture?
fact: Altogether means counting everything together as one total.
fact: Altogether means completely, as in «the noise stopped altogether».
fact: Altogether can mean on the whole, when you think of everything.
fact: In a word problem, altogether is a strong clue that you need to add the parts.
fact: Check your meaning by swapping in a word like completely, in total or on the whole, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The shop sold 3 apples, 4 pears and 2 plums.» How many fruit did the shop sell altogether? | *9 | 5 | 7 | 12
exercise: «The Mole missed the surface altogether.» What does altogether mean here? | On the whole, mostly | Counting everything together | Very slowly | *Completely, not even once
exercise: Ella says, «The trip had some rain, but altogether I loved it.» What does altogether mean here? | Completely, and never again | Very quickly | Counting the rain as one total | *On the whole, when you think of everything
test: Sam has 2 dogs and 3 cats. How many pets does he have altogether? | 3 | 6 | 2 | *5
test: «The noise stopped altogether.» What does altogether mean? | *Completely | On the whole | Counting together | Slowly
exam: Which sentence uses altogether to mean a total? | It was a different thing altogether. | The rain stopped altogether. | *Two bags cost 3 dollars and 4 dollars, so that is 7 dollars altogether. | On the whole, it was fun.
